type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "4 × 200 metres Freestyle Relay, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "10 – 11 August 1936"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/320"} "Schwimmstadion, Reichssportfeld, Berlin"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/5081"} "74 from 18 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Although 18 teams took part in the relay competition, only two teams could realistically challenge for the championship. Once again, it fell between the United States and Japan to fight for gold. Japan fielded three of the first finishers from the individual events and soon moved clear of the Americans to break their own world record and finish ten seconds ahead. " [:a {:href "/athletes/48901"} "Masanori Yusa"] " swam the relay only three times in international competition, breaking the world record each time. Hungary finished clear of France for the bronze medals, with Germany back in fifth."] "\n\n" [:p {} "One of the more remarkable stories in Olympic history concerns French team member " [:a {:href "/athletes/46900"} "Alfred Nakache"] ". Nakache returned to the Olympic arena " [:a {:href "/results/5181"} "12 years later in London"] ", despite having spent time as a prisoner at Auschwitz extermination camp. He is the only known Olympian to have competed before and after internment in a concentration camp."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "Japan"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JPN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JPN.png"}] "JPN"]] [:td {} "8:56.1 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "8:51.5 (1